# Broker access rules. Copy to secrets/mqtt.acl and adapt.
#
# One identity per field node, and a node reaches only its own namespace: it writes
# its telemetry and health (its MQTT last will and its "online" message are health messages, retained) and reads its own
# commands. It can not read another node's data.
# Replace NODE_ID per device; repeat the block for every node.
user field-node-NODE_ID
topic write armor/node/NODE_ID/telemetry
topic write armor/node/NODE_ID/health
# Where the node's own web panel is (its address), for a console to link to.
topic write armor/node/NODE_ID/info
# How the node is doing (uptime, memory, link ...): the same report for every kind of node, armor/health/NODE_ID.
topic write armor/health/NODE_ID
topic read armor/node/NODE_ID/command
# A radar node: the events of its own perimeter engine (QoS 1) and whether the perimeter is armed (only its own subtree).
topic write armor/radar/NODE_ID/events
topic read armor/radar/NODE_ID/control/armed
# Remote control from Studio (restart, pause, standby ...): the node reads its own orders and the ones for every node, and writes its own answer. It can publish no order.
topic read armor/control/NODE_ID/command
topic read armor/control/all/command
topic write armor/control/NODE_ID/result
# Its planned-maintenance signal (retained), only its own topic: it says it is about to be quiet on purpose, and the server holds back the offline alert for a bounded time.
topic write armor/control/NODE_ID/maintenance
# The pins mapped in the node's web panel are devices named after the node (armor/device/NODE_ID/<pin>/state and /set).
topic readwrite armor/device/NODE_ID/#

# The central service observes every node and is the only one allowed to send commands.
user armor-server
topic read armor/node/+/telemetry
topic read armor/node/+/health
topic read armor/node/+/info
# The health report of every kind of node (radar, solar, electrical, alarm, hmi).
topic read armor/health/#
# The events of the perimeter engine of the radar nodes (a zone, a line crossed, a covered sensor, a sensor whose own Bluetooth is open ...).
topic read armor/radar/+/events
# What the solar gateway nodes and the electrical nodes measure (each node writes only its own subtree, see scripts/mqtt_identity.sh).
topic read armor/solar/#
topic read armor/electrical/#
# What the alarm nodes say about their panels and their answers, and the state of the Zigbee zones the server tells them while it holds the radio (information, never a command).
topic read armor/alarm/#
topic write armor/alarm/+/zigbee
# What the network nodes see on the local network.
topic read armor/network/#
topic write armor/node/+/command
# Remote control of the nodes: ONLY the server publishes an order (armor/control/<node>/command and armor/control/all/command) and it reads the answers and the maintenance signals. No node block has a write on an order topic.
topic write armor/control/+/command
topic read armor/control/+/result
topic read armor/control/+/maintenance
# Alarm output: the server publishes alert.raised / alert.cleared / node.offline / node.stale here.
topic write armor/server/alert
# Smart devices (smoke, door, plug, light ...): the server listens on their state and commands them.
topic readwrite armor/device/#

# A solar gateway node (ARMOR-SOLAR, scripts/mqtt_identity.sh add solar-node ID): the readings of the inverters and batteries on its ports; it reads nothing.
# user solar-node-solar-1
# topic write armor/solar/solar-1/#
# topic write armor/health/solar-1
# topic read armor/control/solar-1/command
# topic read armor/control/all/command
# topic write armor/control/solar-1/result
# topic write armor/control/solar-1/maintenance
#
# An electrical node (ARMOR-ELECTRICAL, scripts/mqtt_identity.sh add electrical-node ID): it writes what it measures and its answers, and reads nothing.
# user electrical-node-electrical-1
# topic write armor/electrical/electrical-1/state
# topic write armor/electrical/electrical-1/result
# topic write armor/health/electrical-1
# topic read armor/control/electrical-1/command
# topic read armor/control/all/command
# topic write armor/control/electrical-1/result
# topic write armor/control/electrical-1/maintenance
#
# A command to a node's switch is refused by the broker unless BOTH of these lines exist, and only `scripts/mqtt_identity.sh electrical-switching ID on` writes them
# (`off` removes them). The server also needs ARMOR_ELECTRICAL_SWITCHING=1 and the node has to allow switching itself: three independent switches, all off by default.
#   in the block of electrical-node-electrical-1:   topic read armor/electrical/electrical-1/command
#   in the block of armor-server:                   topic write armor/electrical/electrical-1/command

# An alarm node (ARMOR-ALARM, scripts/mqtt_identity.sh add alarm-node ID): it writes the state of its panel and its answers, and reads only the state of its Zigbee zones.
# user alarm-node-alarm-1
# topic write armor/alarm/alarm-1/state
# topic write armor/alarm/alarm-1/result
# topic write armor/health/alarm-1
# topic read armor/control/alarm-1/command
# topic read armor/control/all/command
# topic write armor/control/alarm-1/result
# topic write armor/control/alarm-1/maintenance
# topic read armor/alarm/alarm-1/zigbee
#
# A command to a panel (arm, disarm) is refused by the broker unless BOTH of these lines exist, and only `scripts/mqtt_identity.sh alarm-commands ID on` adds them (`off` removes them). The
# server also needs ARMOR_ALARM_COMMANDS=1 and the node has to allow it itself (server.commands): three independent switches. The server asks for the PIN of the signed-in user before it sends a disarm
# (the PIN never goes to the node), so keep them as strict as you want it.
#   in the block of alarm-node-alarm-1:   topic read armor/alarm/alarm-1/command
#   in the block of armor-server:         topic write armor/alarm/alarm-1/command
#
# The relays of an electrical node, or the siren relay and the others of an alarm node, are devices of the device layer: they are reachable only after
# `scripts/mqtt_identity.sh electrical-relays ID on` or `alarm-relays ID on`, which add to the node's block:   topic readwrite armor/device/ID/#
#
# Home Assistant (ARMOR-SERVER with ARMOR_HA_DISCOVERY=1) reads the same broker and only needs to read what the nodes publish, and to be able to read its own discovery topics:
# user home-assistant
# topic read armor/#
# topic read homeassistant/#
# and, in the block of armor-server, so that it may describe the entities (retained):   topic write homeassistant/#
#
# A network node (ARMOR-NETWORK, scripts/mqtt_identity.sh add network-node ID): it writes what it sees on the local network and reads nothing.
# user network-node-network-1
# topic write armor/network/network-1/state

# An alarm consumer (a siren controller, a notifier) only reads alerts.
# user alarm-consumer
# topic read armor/server/alert

# One smart device that speaks MQTT itself (a Tasmota plug, an ESP32 sensor) reaches only its own topics.
# user device-kitchen
# topic readwrite armor/device/kitchen/#

# A bridge to many devices (Zigbee2MQTT with base_topic armor/device, a Shelly gateway) needs all of them.
# user bridge-zigbee
# topic readwrite armor/device/#
